r(--thread-wide-block-inline-shift)); margin-inline: auto;" class="relative flex flex-1 shrink-0 flex-col transcriptContent-l_FOQD [--thread-content-max-width:var(--thread-content-responsive-max-width,inherit)] [--thread-body-max-width:calc(var(--thread-content-max-width)+var(--thread-body-inline-padding,0px)*2)] mx-auto w-full max-w-(--thread-body-max-width) px-[var(--thread-body-inline-padding,var(--padding-toolbar))] pb-8 has-[[data-compact-status-slot-clearance]]:pb-0" data-mcp-app-portal-target="true"><div data-chatgpt-conversation-selection-target="6af6bb74-0c20-83ee-b54b-48409e00f501" class="flex min-h-full flex-1 flex-col gap-1.5 pb-5" data-thread-find-target="conversation" style="transform: none;"><div class="relative shrink-0" style="height: 13570px;"><div class="flex flex-col" style="margin-top: 0px;"><div class="" style="height: 10200px; margin-top: 0px; contain: size;"><div class="[&amp;_[data-virtualized-turn-content]]:[content-visibility:visible]" data-turn-key="1bd9ed71-818b-43c1-9995-98b13af48e95"><div data-content-search-turn-key="fallback-turn-0"><div class="flex flex-col gap-1.5"><div class="group flex flex-col" data-talvt-turn-state="complete"><div class="flex flex-col gap-3 browser:gap-1"><div class="block-BQZwFn"><div class="group/user-message flex flex-col items-end gap-2" data-chatgpt-search-unit-key="fallback-turn-0:0:user" data-chatgpt-search-message-ids="1bd9ed71-818b-43c1-9995-98b13af48e95"><div class="w-full" data-content-search-unit-key="fallback-turn-0:0:user"><div class="group/user-message flex w-full flex-col gap-1 print:group-data-[print-formatting]/conversation-print:items-start items-end"><div data-user-message-bubble="true" class="bg-user-message text-user-message min-w-0 max-w-(--user-chat-width) overflow-hidden break-words px-(--thread-content-margin) [&amp;_.contain-inline-size]:[contain:initial] bubble-vBnidZ py-2.5 rounded-2xl relative text-start"><div class="flex flex-col items-end gap-1"><div class="relative w-full min-w-0 text-size-chat leading-relaxed"><div class="" style="" data-search-result-target=""><div><div class="text-size-chat whitespace-pre-wrap" dir="auto"># Warpgate bastion and container browser

A local bastion for the corporate VPN, plus a containerised browser that runs
inside that VPN. The point of the layout is that **the VPN lives only inside the
pods**: the containers reach the corporate network through the tunnel, while the
host keeps its own internet connection. You can browse the internet and the
corporate intranet at the same time.

## How it works

<pre class="my-2 max-w-full min-w-0 overflow-x-auto rounded-xl bg-(--color-text-user-message)/10 p-2 font-code text-size-code whitespace-pre text-user-message" data-horizontal-scroll="true" data-markdown-copy="code-block" dir="ltr"><code data-markdown-copy-content="true">host ──────────────────────────────► internet
  │
  │  (no tun device, no VPN routes on the host)
  │
  └─► podman network "warpgate_default"
        │
        └─► warpgate-vpn  ──► tun0 ──► corporate VPN
              │  ▲
              │  └── network namespace shared by the two pods below
              │
              ├─► warpgate  (SSH + HTTP bastion)
              └─► firefox  (Kasm image, browser over VNC)</code></pre>

<code>warpgate</code> and <code>warpgate-firefox</code> use <code>network_mode: "service:vpn"</code>, so they
join the **VPN container's network namespace**. The tunnel interface lives in
that namespace and nowhere else, which is what keeps the host's routing table
untouched. Everything the two pods send goes out through the tunnel.

The VPN container also runs <code>socat</code>, forwarding TCP 443 on that namespace to
the corporate bastion address, so the pods can reach the OpenShift cluster
through the VPN on the usual HTTPS port.

## Files

| File | Purpose |
| --- | --- |
| <code>podman-compose.yml</code> | The real stack, using the corporate gateway. |
| <code>podman-compose.test.yml</code> | The same stack against a local OpenVPN server, for testing without corporate credentials. |
| <code>Dockerfile</code> | Builds <code>localhost/warpgate-vpn:latest</code> (openconnect, openvpn, socat). |
| <code>Dockerfile.firefox</code> | Builds <code>localhost/warpgate-firefox:latest</code> from the Kasm Firefox image. |
| <code>firefox-entrypoint.sh</code> | Entrypoint for the browser container, see "Fixes" below. |
| <code>vpn_entrypoint.sh</code> | Keeps the VPN up and runs the bastion forward. |
| <code>test/</code> | Local OpenVPN server used by the test harness. |
| <code>secrets/vpn_password</code> | The corporate password, read only by the VPN container. |
| <code>data/</code> | Warpgate's state (database, TLS certificate). |

## Requirements

- Podman and podman-compose
- Rootless Podman with a usable <code>newuidmap</code>/<code>newgidmap</code>, and your user
  listed in <code>/etc/subuid</code> and <code>/etc/subgid</code>
- SELinux enforcing is fine, the containers are labelled <code>spc_t</code>

## The password

The corporate password is read from <code>secrets/vpn_password</code>, which is mounted
read-only into the VPN container only. It is never passed as an environment
variable, so it does not show up in <code>podman inspect</code> or in <code>/proc</code>.

Create it with:

<pre class="my-2 max-w-full min-w-0 overflow-x-auto rounded-xl bg-(--color-text-user-message)/10 p-2 font-code text-size-code whitespace-pre text-user-message" data-horizontal-scroll="true" data-markdown-copy="code-block" dir="ltr"><code data-language="bash" data-markdown-copy-content="true">install -m 0600 /dev/null secrets/vpn_password
printf '%s' 'THE-CORPORATE-PASSWORD' &gt; secrets/vpn_password</code></pre>

Nothing else needs the password. Do not commit this file.

## Running it

<pre class="my-2 max-w-full min-w-0 overflow-x-auto rounded-xl bg-(--color-text-user-message)/10 p-2 font-code text-size-code whitespace-pre text-user-message" data-horizontal-scroll="true" data-markdown-copy="code-block" dir="ltr"><code data-language="bash" data-markdown-copy-content="true"># Build the images.
podman build -t localhost/warpgate-vpn:latest .
podman build -t localhost/warpgate-firefox:latest -f Dockerfile.firefox .

# Start the stack.
podman-compose up -d

# Check that the tunnel came up.
podman exec warpgate-vpn ip -br addr show tun0

# Check the bastion.
curl -sk https://127.0.0.1:8888/            # HTTP bastion, expects an auth prompt
ssh -p 2222 &lt;user&gt;@127.0.0.1                 # SSH bastion

# The browser, over VNC.
# VNC password is VNC_PW, so "testpass123" as configured.</code></pre>

Useful addresses once it is running:

| Address | What |
| --- | --- |
| <code>127.0.0.1:8888</code> | Warpgate HTTP bastion |
| <code>127.0.0.1:2222</code> | Warpgate SSH bastion |
| <code>127.0.0.1:443</code> | Forward to the corporate bastion through the tunnel |
| <code>127.0.0.1:6901</code> | The browser's VNC endpoint |

## Testing without the corporate VPN

<code>podman-compose.test.yml</code> runs the same stack against a local OpenVPN server
that stands in for the corporate gateway. It needs no corporate password and
never touches <code>vpn.osdc.gov.in</code>.

<pre class="my-2 max-w-full min-w-0 overflow-x-auto rounded-xl bg-(--color-text-user-message)/10 p-2 font-code text-size-code whitespace-pre text-user-message" data-horizontal-scroll="true" data-markdown-copy="code-block" dir="ltr"><code data-language="bash" data-markdown-copy-content="true"># Build the test server image.
podman build -t localhost/ovpn-test-server:latest -f test/Dockerfile.ovpn-server test/

# Build the main images as well.
podman build -t localhost/warpgate-vpn:latest .
podman build -t localhost/warpgate-firefox:latest -f Dockerfile.firefox .

# Start the test stack.
podman-compose -f podman-compose.test.yml up -d

# The tunnel is up when the tun0 interface appears.
podman exec warpgate-vpn-test ip -br addr show tun0

# The stand-in corporate resource is reachable through the tunnel.
podman exec warpgate-firefox-test \
    curl -s --noproxy '*' http://127.0.0.1:443/

# The host still has its own internet.
curl -s -o /dev/null -w '%{http_code}\n' https://example.com    # 200

# Tear down, including the generated PKI volume.
podman-compose -f podman-compose.test.yml down -v</code></pre>

The test server generates its own throwaway CA and certificates on first
start and serves a marker page on the far side of the tunnel, so the test
proves that traffic really flows through the tunnel rather than merely being
configured.

## Fixes that were needed to make this work

These were all found by running the stack and reading the container logs.
They are already applied, and are worth knowing about if the images are ever
rebuilt from scratch.

**SELinux blocked the tunnel device.** The host's <code>/dev/net/tun</code> is owned by
root, so a rootless container cannot open it unless SELinux allows it. The
containers are labelled <code>spc_t</code>, the "super privileged container" type that a
VPN container needs. Without it the VPN fails with:

<pre class="my-2 max-w-full min-w-0 overflow-x-auto rounded-xl bg-(--color-text-user-message)/10 p-2 font-code text-size-code whitespace-pre text-user-message" data-horizontal-scroll="true" data-markdown-copy="code-block" dir="ltr"><code data-markdown-copy-content="true">mknod: /dev/net/tun: File exists
Failed to open tun device: Permission denied</code></pre>

**<code>userns: keep-id</code> is required.** Without it the container's root is an
unmapped subuid and the <code>TUNSETIFF</code> ioctl is rejected with <code>EPERM</code>.

**The browser container must not run as root.** The original
<code>Dockerfile.firefox</code> had <code>USER root</code> so that the <code>sed</code> patch could be
applied. That makes Firefox refuse to start, because <code>HOME</code> belongs to
<code>kasm-user</code>:

<pre class="my-2 max-w-full min-w-0 overflow-x-auto rounded-xl bg-(--color-text-user-message)/10 p-2 font-code text-size-code whitespace-pre text-user-message" data-horizontal-scroll="true" data-markdown-copy="code-block" dir="ltr"><code data-markdown-copy-content="true">Running Firefox as root in a regular user's session is not supported.</code></pre>

The patch is now applied in a separate build stage, and the final image keeps
the original uid 1000.

**The browser container needs a TTY.** <code>kasmvncpasswd</code> calls <code>isatty</code> and
<code>tcgetattr</code>, so it fails without one:

<pre class="my-2 max-w-full min-w-0 overflow-x-auto rounded-xl bg-(--color-text-user-message)/10 p-2 font-code text-size-code whitespace-pre text-user-message" data-horizontal-scroll="true" data-markdown-copy="code-block" dir="ltr"><code data-markdown-copy-content="true">getpassword error: Inappropriate ioctl for device</code></pre>

**The browser container needs its hostname resolvable.** The Kasm startup
script aborts if <code>hostname</code> cannot be resolved. A container that joins
another container's network namespace does not get its own hostname added to
<code>/etc/hosts</code> by Podman, so <code>firefox-entrypoint.sh</code> adds it before dropping
privileges. <code>/etc/hosts</code> is a root-owned tmpfs, so that has to happen while
still root.

**The published ports belong to the <code>vpn</code> service.** <code>warpgate</code> and
<code>firefox</code> share the VPN container's namespace, so any <code>ports:</code> for them would
not be published. The port list belongs on <code>vpn</code>.

**Rootless Podman cannot bind port 443.** Binding a privileged port needs
<code>net.ipv4.ip_unprivileged_port_start=443</code> in <code>sysctl.conf</code>. The test harness
publishes that hop as 8443 instead; the real stack can use 443 once the
sysctl is set.

## Checking that the isolation holds

<pre class="my-2 max-w-full min-w-0 overflow-x-auto rounded-xl bg-(--color-text-user-message)/10 p-2 font-code text-size-code whitespace-pre text-user-message" data-horizontal-scroll="true" data-markdown-copy="code-block" dir="ltr"><code data-language="bash" data-markdown-copy-content="true"># The host has no tunnel interface.
ip -br addr | grep tun             # no output

# The host has no routes into the corporate network.
ip route | grep -E '10\.(8|250)\.'   # no output

# The host still reaches the internet.
curl -s -o /dev/null -w '%{http_code}\n' https://example.com   # 200

# The pods do reach the corporate network.
podman exec warpgate-firefox curl -s --noproxy '*' http://127.0.0.1:443/</code></pre>

## Troubleshooting

**The VPN container logs <code>Failed to open tun device</code>.** The SELinux label or
the user namespace is wrong. Check that the service has both
<code>security_opt: label=type:spc_t</code> and <code>userns: keep-id</code>.

**The browser container restarts in a loop.** Check the last lines of its log:

<pre class="my-2 max-w-full min-w-0 overflow-x-auto rounded-xl bg-(--color-text-user-message)/10 p-2 font-code text-size-code whitespace-pre text-user-message" data-horizontal-scroll="true" data-markdown-copy="code-block" dir="ltr"><code data-language="bash" data-markdown-copy-content="true">podman logs --tail 20 warpgate-firefox</code></pre>

- <code>Running Firefox as root in a regular user's session is not supported</code>
  means the image is running as root. Rebuild <code>Dockerfile.firefox</code>.
- <code>hostname: Name or service not known</code> means the hostname is not resolvable.
  Rebuild the image so <code>firefox-entrypoint.sh</code> is in place.
- <code>getpassword error: Inappropriate ioctl for device</code> means the container has
  no TTY.

**The tunnel comes up but nothing is reachable.** Check that <code>warpgate</code> and
<code>firefox</code> really share the namespace:

<pre class="my-2 max-w-full min-w-0 overflow-x-auto rounded-xl bg-(--color-text-user-message)/10 p-2 font-code text-size-code whitespace-pre text-user-message" data-horizontal-scroll="true" data-markdown-copy="code-block" dir="ltr"><code data-language="bash" data-markdown-copy-content="true">for c in warpgate-vpn warpgate warpgate-firefox; do
    pid=$(podman inspect "$c" --format '{{.State.Pid}}')
    echo "$c $(readlink /proc/$pid/ns/net)"
done</code></pre>

All three must print the same <code>net:[...]</code>. If they differ, the pods were
started before the VPN container and are in a stale namespace. Recreate them
with <code>podman-compose up -d --force-recreate</code>.

**Warpgate says <code>Failed to construct external URL for cookie domain</code>.** Only a
warning. It means <code>external_host</code> is not set in <code>data/warpgate.yaml</code>, so
cookies are scoped to the request host. Set <code>external_host</code> to the address
you actually use.

explain me this what the AI built last night. I'll explain what I had done and you explain what AI had built</div></div></div></div></div></div><div class="flex flex-row-reverse items-center gap-1"><div class="isolate me-1 ms-1 flex items-center gap-2 opacity-0 group-has-[:focus-visible]/user-message:opacity-100 group-hover/user-message:opacity-100 [@media(hover:none)]:opacity-100"><div class="flex turn-action-controls items-center gap-0.5 browser:gap-0"></div></div></div></div></div></div></div><div class="block-BQZwFn"><div data-content-search-unit-key="fallback-turn-0:2:assistant" data-chatgpt-search-unit-key="fallback-turn-0:2:assistant" data-chatgpt-search-message-ids="2f68e306-af27-4774-a476-b55566df6c73 2f68e306-af27-4774-a476-b55566df6c73"><div><div class="group flex min-w-0 flex-col" data-chatgpt-selection-conversation-id="6af6bb74-0c20-83ee-b54b-48409e00f501" data-chatgpt-selection-message-id="2f68e306-af27-4774-a476-b55566df6c73"><div data-selected-text-overlay-target="_r_bj_" dir="auto" data-markdown-text-style="assistant-message" class="MarkdownRoot-rZKhxa [&amp;&gt;*:first-child]:mt-0 [&amp;&gt;*:last-child]:mb-0 [&amp;&gt;*:last-child]:mb-0 [&amp;&gt;ol:first-child]:mt-0 [&amp;&gt;ul:first-child]:mt-0" style="--markdown-link-color: currentColor;"><div class="my-4 first:mt-0 last:mb-0 empty:hidden" data-dil-source-message-id="2f68e306-af27-4774-a476-b55566df6c73" data-chatgpt-copy-reference="0" data-markdown-copy="contents"><div class="[--entity-prose-inset:0px]"><div class="relative PortalBoundary-TFj9W2 Renderer-ojZscX" data-theme="dark" style="--puik-portal-zoom: 1;"><div dir="auto" class="DilRenderer-tB76Jj DilResponseRoot-HfQrEh Reveal-k2IWvM" data-dil-response="" data-d-direction="col" data-dil-message-id="2f68e306-af27-4774-a476-b55566df6c73"><p class="TextBase-hXhSqg Text-lIMP0_" data-d-text-flow="" data-d-component="text">Dude. 😂 You apparently asked an AI to solve a corporate VPN access problem, and it responded by building a <span class="TextBase-hXhSqg Text-lIMP0_" data-d-text-flow="" data-d-component="text" data-d-default-strong="" data-d-inline=""><strong class="Emphasis-Sdofse">container-isolated VPN, an SSH/HTTP bastion, and a remotely accessible Firefox browser</strong></span>, all running under rootless Podman.</p><p class="TextBase-hXhSqg Text-lIMP0_" data-d-text-flow="" data-d-component="text">That's considerably more sophisticated than simply running OpenConnect on your laptop.</p><p class="TextBase-hXhSqg Text-lIMP0_" data-d-text-flow="" data-d-component="text">And the interesting part is that the architecture is actually sensible. There are some security and networking details I'd scrutinize, though.</p><p class="TextBase-hXhSqg Text-lIMP0_" data-d-text-flow="" data-d-component="text">I'll explain what it built, but first I want to understand the distinction between <span class="TextBase-hXhSqg Text-lIMP0_" data-d-text-flow="" data-d-component="text" data-d-default-strong="" data-d-inline=""><strong class="Emphasis-Sdofse">what you wanted</strong></span> and <span class="TextBase-hXhSqg Text-lIMP0_" data-d-text-flow="" data-d-component="text" data-d-default-strong="" data-d-inline=""><strong class="Emphasis-Sdofse">what the AI implemented</strong></span>.</p><h2 class="TextBase-hXhSqg Title-HRvkwB" data-d-text-flow="" data-d-component="title" data-d-size="lg" data-d-weight="semibold"><strong class="Emphasis-Sdofse">What the AI built, at a glance</strong></h2><div class="Box-qIMv6G" data-d-component="box" data-d-direction="col" data-d-has-border="" style="border: 1px solid var(--color-border); border-radius: var(--dil-radius-xl); --w-box-gutter-block-end: calc(var(--spacing, 0.25rem) * 3); --w-box-gutter-block-start: calc(var(--spacing, 0.25rem) * 3); --w-box-gutter-inline-end: calc(var(--spacing, 0.25rem) * 3); --w-box-gutter-inline-start: calc(var(--spacing, 0.25rem) * 3); padding-block: calc(var(--spacing, 0.25rem) * 3); padding-inline: calc(var(--spacing, 0.25rem) * 3); gap: calc(var(--spacing, 0.25rem) * 3);"><img alt="" data-d-component="svg" decoding="async" style="aspect-ratio: auto 340 / 270; color: var(--color-text-prose); width: 100%;" src="data:image/svg+xml;charset=utf-8,%3Csvg%20font-family%3D%22-apple-system-body%2C%20ui-sans-serif%2C%20-apple-system%2C%20system-ui%2C%20Segoe%20UI%2C%20Helvetica%2C%20Apple%20Color%20Emoji%2C%20Arial%2C%20sans-serif%2C%20Segoe%20UI%20Emoji%2C%20Segoe%20UI%20Symbol%22%20font-weight%3D%22400%22%20data-d-component%3D%22svg%22%20fill%3D%22currentColor%22%20style%3D%22color%3Argb(237%2C%20237%2C%20237)%22%20viewBox%3D%220%200%20340%20270%22%20width%3D%22100%25%22%20xmlns%3D%22http%3A%2F%2Fwww.w3.org%2F2000%2Fsvg%22%3E%3Crect%20x%3D%224%22%20y%3D%224%22%20width%3D%22332%22%20height%3D%22262%22%20rx%3D%2212%22%20stroke%3D%22currentColor%22%20stroke-opacity%3D%22.2%22%20fill%3D%22none%22%2F%3E%3Crect%20x%3D%2219%22%20y%3D%2217%22%20width%3D%22302%22%20height%3D%2242%22%20rx%3D%228%22%20fill%3D%22%2364748b%22%20fill-opacity%3D%22.13%22%20stroke%3D%22%2364748b%22%2F%3E%3Ctext%20x%3D%22170%22%20y%3D%2234%22%20font-size%3D%2212%22%20text-anchor%3D%22middle%22%20fill%3D%22currentColor%22%20font-weight%3D%22600%22%3EYour%20Linux%20host%3C%2Ftext%3E%3Ctext%20x%3D%22170%22%20y%3D%2249%22%20font-size%3D%229%22%20text-anchor%3D%22middle%22%20fill%3D%22currentColor%22%3ENormal%20internet%2C%20no%20host%20VPN%20routes%3C%2Ftext%3E%3Cpath%20d%3D%22M170%2059%20V83%22%20stroke%3D%22%2364748b%22%20stroke-width%3D%221.8%22%2F%3E%3Cpath%20d%3D%22M166%2078%20L170%2084%20L174%2078%22%20stroke%3D%22%2364748b%22%20fill%3D%22none%22%2F%3E%3Crect%20x%3D%2218%22%20y%3D%2286%22%20width%3D%22304%22%20height%3D%22162%22%20rx%3D%229%22%20stroke%3D%22%230d9488%22%20stroke-width%3D%221.5%22%20stroke-dasharray%3D%225%204%22%20fill%3D%22%230d9488%22%20fill-opacity%3D%22.05%22%2F%3E%3Ctext%20x%3D%22170%22%20y%3D%22102%22%20font-size%3D%2210%22%20text-anchor%3D%22middle%22%20fill%3D%22%230d9488%22%20font-weight%3D%22600%22%3EShared%20container%20network%20namespace%3C%2Ftext%3E%3Crect%20x%3D%2230%22%20y%3D%22120%22%20width%3D%2292%22%20height%3D%2252%22%20rx%3D%227%22%20fill%3D%22%230d9488%22%20fill-opacity%3D%22.13%22%20stroke%3D%22%230d9488%22%2F%3E%3Ctext%20x%3D%2276%22%20y%3D%22140%22%20font-size%3D%2211%22%20text-anchor%3D%22middle%22%20fill%3D%22currentColor%22%20font-weight%3D%22600%22%3EVPN%3C%2Ftext%3E%3Ctext%20x%3D%2276%22%20y%3D%22156%22%20font-size%3D%229%22%20text-anchor%3D%22middle%22%20fill%3D%22currentColor%22%3EOpenConnect%3C%2Ftext%3E%3Crect%20x%3D%22124%22%20y%3D%22120%22%20width%3D%2292%22%20height%3D%2252%22%20rx%3D%227%22%20fill%3D%22%2364748b%22%20fill-opacity%3D%22.13%22%20stroke%3D%22%2364748b%22%2F%3E%3Ctext%20x%3D%22170%22%20y%3D%22140%22%20font-size%3D%2211%22%20text-anchor%3D%22middle%22%20fill%3D%22currentColor%22%20font-weight%3D%22600%22%3EWarpgate%3C%2Ftext%3E%3Ctext%20x%3D%22170%22%20y%3D%22156%22%20font-size%3D%229%22%20text-anchor%3D%22middle%22%20fill%3D%22currentColor%22%3ESSH%20%2B%20HTTPS%3C%2Ftext%3E%3Crect%20x%3D%22218%22%20y%3D%22120%22%20width%3D%2292%22%20height%3D%2252%22%20rx%3D%227%22%20fill%3D%22%2364748b%22%20fill-opacity%3D%22.13%22%20stroke%3D%22%2364748b%22%2F%3E%3Ctext%20x%3D%22264%22%20y%3D%22140%22%20font-size%3D%2211%22%20text-anchor%3D%22middle%22%20fill%3D%22currentColor%22%20font-weight%3D%22600%22%3EFirefox%3C%2Ftext%3E%3Ctext%20x%3D%22264%22%20y%3D%22156%22%20font-size%3D%229%22%20text-anchor%3D%22middle%22%20fill%3D%22currentColor%22%3EKasmVNC%3C%2Ftext%3E%3Cpath%20d%3D%22M76%20172%20V191%20H170%20V204%22%20fill%3D%22none%22%20stroke%3D%22%230d9488%22%20stroke-width%3D%221.7%22%2F%3E%3Crect%20x%3D%2288%22%20y%3D%22205%22%20width%3D%22164%22%20height%3D%2229%22%20rx%3D%227%22%20fill%3D%22%230d9488%22%20fill-opacity%3D%22.15%22%20stroke%3D%22%230d9488%22%2F%3E%3Ctext%20x%3D%22170%22%20y%3D%22223%22%20font-size%3D%2211%22%20font-weight%3D%22600%22%20text-anchor%3D%22middle%22%20fill%3D%22currentColor%22%3ECorporate%20VPN%20tunnel%20(tun0)%3C%2Ftext%3E%3Cpath%20d%3D%22M122%20146%20H124%20M216%20146%20H218%22%20stroke%3D%22%230d9488%22%20stroke-width%3D%222%22%20stroke-dasharray%3D%223%202%22%2F%3E%3Ctext%20x%3D%22170%22%20y%3D%22260%22%20font-size%3D%229%22%20text-anchor%3D%22middle%22%20fill%3D%22currentColor%22%3EVPN%20interface%20exists%20inside%20containers%20only%3C%2Ftext%3E%3C%2Fsvg%3E"><span class="TextBase-hXhSqg Caption-VSNpzV" data-d-text-flow="" data-d-component="caption" data-d-size="lg" data-d-weight="normal">Conceptual layout, not three independent container networks. The VPN, Warpgate and Firefox share one network namespace.</span></div><h2 class="TextBase-hXhSqg Title-HRvkwB" data-d-text-flow="" data-d-component="title" data-d-size="lg" data-d-weight="semibold"><strong class="Emphasis-Sdofse">The three major components</strong></h2><p class="TextBase-hXhSqg Text-lIMP0_" data-d-text-flow="" data-d-component="text"><span class="TextBase-hXhSqg Text-lIMP0_" data-d-text-flow="" data-d-component="text" data-d-default-strong="" data-d-inline=""><strong class="Emphasis-Sdofse">1. </strong><code class="Code-kbs71c" data-d-component="code">warpgate-vpn</code><strong class="Emphasis-Sdofse">: The network gateway</strong></span></p><p class="TextBase-hXhSqg Text-lIMP0_" data-d-text-flow="" data-d-component="text">This container runs OpenConnect, establishes the corporate VPN, and creates <code class="Code-kbs71c" data-d-component="code">tun0</code> inside its own network namespace.</p><p class="TextBase-hXhSqg Text-lIMP0_" data-d-text-flow="" data-d-component="text">Your host doesn't need a VPN tunnel or corporate routing entries. It continues using your normal internet connection.</p><p class="TextBase-hXhSqg Text-lIMP0_" data-d-text-flow="" data-d-component="text"><span class="TextBase-hXhSqg Text-lIMP0_" data-d-text-flow="" data-d-component="text" data-d-default-strong="" data-d-inline=""><strong class="Emphasis-Sdofse">2. </strong><code class="Code-kbs71c" data-d-component="code">warpgate</code><strong class="Emphasis-Sdofse">: The bastion</strong></span></p><p class="TextBase-hXhSqg Text-lIMP0_" data-d-text-flow="" data-d-component="text"><span class="Box-qIMv6G Pressable-KyqH6M" data-d-component="pressable" data-d-direction="col" data-d-inline="" role="button" style="gap: calc(var(--spacing, 0.25rem) * 0); display: inline;" tabindex="0"><span class="TextBase-hXhSqg Text-lIMP0_" data-d-text-flow="" data-d-component="text" data-d-inline="" data-d-text-decoration="underline-dotted">Warpgate</span></span> acts as an authenticated gateway to internal systems.</p><p class="TextBase-hXhSqg Text-lIMP0_" data-d-text-flow="" data-d-component="text">Instead of directly connecting your host to internal SSH or web services, you connect to the locally published Warpgate ports, and Warpgate makes the onward connections from inside the VPN network.</p><p class="TextBase-hXhSqg Text-lIMP0_" data-d-text-flow="" data-d-component="text"><span class="TextBase-hXhSqg Text-lIMP0_" data-d-text-flow="" data-d-component="text" data-d-default-strong="" data-d-inline=""><strong class="Emphasis-Sdofse">3. </strong><code class="Code-kbs71c" data-d-component="code">warpgate-firefox</code><strong class="Emphasis-Sdofse">: The browser</strong></span></p><p class="TextBase-hXhSqg Text-lIMP0_" data-d-text-flow="" data-d-component="text">This is an actual Firefox instance running inside a container, using KasmVNC to present its graphical interface to your host browser.</p><p class="TextBase-hXhSqg Text-lIMP0_" data-d-text-flow="" data-d-component="text">That means you're effectively controlling a second browser whose network connections originate inside the VPN namespace.</p><p class="TextBase-hXhSqg Text-lIMP0_" data-d-text-flow="" data-d-component="text">For example, your normal host Firefox can visit YouTube while the containerized Firefox visits your internal OpenShift console.</p><h2 class="TextBase-hXhSqg Title-HRvkwB" data-d-text-flow="" data-d-component="title" data-d-size="lg" data-d-weight="semibold"><strong class="Emphasis-Sdofse">The clever part: </strong><code class="Code-kbs71c" data-d-component="code">network_mode: service:vpn</code></h2><p class="TextBase-hXhSqg Text-lIMP0_" data-d-text-flow="" data-d-component="text">This is the architectural centerpiece.</p><p class="TextBase-hXhSqg Text-lIMP0_" data-d-text-flow="" data-d-component="text">Normally, separate containers have separate network namespaces. Here, the AI configured Warpgate and Firefox to join the VPN container's namespace.</p><p class="TextBase-hXhSqg Text-lIMP0_" data-d-text-flow="" data-d-component="text">Consequently, all three see the same <code class="Code-kbs71c" data-d-component="code">tun0</code>, interfaces, IP addresses, routing table, and listening TCP ports.</p><p class="TextBase-hXhSqg Text-lIMP0_" data-d-text-flow="" data-d-component="text">No need to run three VPN clients. No need to modify your host routing table.</p><p class="TextBase-hXhSqg Text-lIMP0_" data-d-text-flow="" data-d-component="text">There is one important nuance, though: <span class="TextBase-hXhSqg Text-lIMP0_" data-d-text-flow="" data-d-component="text" data-d-default-strong="" data-d-inline=""><strong class="Emphasis-Sdofse">sharing the VPN namespace does not automatically mean all traffic goes through the VPN.</strong></span> That depends on the routes OpenConnect installs. With a split-tunnel configuration, corporate destinations can use <code class="Code-kbs71c" data-d-component="code">tun0</code> while public internet traffic exits through the container's ordinary network connection. A full-tunnel configuration behaves differently.</p><p class="TextBase-hXhSqg Text-lIMP0_" data-d-text-flow="" data-d-component="text">Also, <code class="Code-kbs71c" data-d-component="code">spc_t</code> is a broad SELinux concession, and the hardcoded VNC password in the documentation should not be treated as production-safe. The claimed password isolation and routing isolation are worth checking against the actual Compose files rather than accepting the README as proof.</p><div class="Divider-WkGDyu" data-d-component="divider" style="--divider-color: var(--color-border); --divider-size: 1px;"></div><h2 class="TextBase-hXhSqg Title-HRvkwB" data-d-text-flow="" data-d-component="title" data-d-size="lg" data-d-weight="semibold"><strong class="Emphasis-Sdofse">Now, tell me what you actually did</strong></h2><p class="TextBase-hXhSqg Text-lIMP0_" data-d-text-flow="" data-d-component="text">I'm particularly interested in <span class="TextBase-hXhSqg Text-lIMP0_" data-d-text-flow="" data-d-component="text" data-d-default-strong="" data-d-inline=""><strong class="Emphasis-Sdofse">the original problem you gave the AI</strong></span>.</p><p class="TextBase-hXhSqg Text-lIMP0_" data-d-text-flow="" data-d-component="text">Were you originally trying to access your corporate OpenShift console, internal servers, and websites through the government/corporate OpenConnect VPN, without allowing that VPN to take over your Fedora or NixOS host's networking?</p><p class="TextBase-hXhSqg Text-lIMP0_" data-d-text-flow="" data-d-component="text">Explain your original setup, your requirements, and what you told the AI to build.</p><p class="TextBase-hXhSqg Text-lIMP0_" data-d-text-flow="" data-d-component="text">Then I'll connect your requirements to each component it created, explain the packet flow and Linux network namespaces in detail, and distinguish the genuinely clever engineering from things the AI might have overengineered. Because, apparently, it spent last night building a miniature zero-trust access platform on your laptop. 💀</p></div></div></div><div class="contents"></div></div></div></div></div></div></div></div><div class="actionRow-nqqZbm mt-1.5 flex turn-action-controls min-h-5 min-w-0 max-w-(--thread-content-max-width) flex-wrap items-center justify-start gap-0.5 browser:mt-3 [&amp;_button]:focus-visible:ring-2 [&amp;_button]:focus-visible:ring-ring [&amp;_button]:focus-visible:ring-offset-0 browser:-ms-0.5 electron:-translate-x-1 extension:-translate-x-1.5 [--side-tooltip:bottom]"><div class="flex min-h-5 min-w-0 flex-wrap items-center gap-0.5 browser:gap-0"><span class="LeadingSource-d79mNZ" aria-hidden="true"></span></div></div></div></div></div></div></div><div class="" style="contain: size; height: 2840px; margin-top: 12px;"><div class="[&amp;_[data-virtualized-turn-content]]:[content-visibility:visible]" data-turn-key="1334b17a-4590-4d6a-9d5b-1411132f40ba"><div data-content-search-turn-key="fallback-turn-1"><div class="flex flex-col gap-1.5"><div class="group flex flex-col" data-talvt-turn-state="complete"><div class="flex flex-col gap-3 browser:gap-1"><div class="block-BQZwFn"><div class="group/user-message flex flex-col items-end gap-2" data-chatgpt-search-unit-key="fallback-turn-1:0:user" data-chatgpt-search-message-ids="1334b17a-4590-4d6a-9d5b-1411132f40ba"><div class="w-full" data-content-search-unit-key="fallback-turn-1:0:user"><div class="group/user-message flex w-full flex-col gap-1 print:group-data-[print-formatting]/conversation-print:items-start items-end"><div data-user-message-bubble="true" class="bg-user-message text-user-message min-w-0 max-w-(--user-chat-width) overflow-hidden break-words px-(--thread-content-margin) [&amp;_.contain-inline-size]:[contain:initial] bubble-vBnidZ py-2.5 rounded-2xl relative text-start"><div class="flex flex-col items-end gap-1"><div class="relative w-full min-w-0 text-size-chat leading-relaxed"><div class="" data-search-result-target=""><div><div class="text-size-chat whitespace-pre-wrap" dir="auto">No actually Idea was mine and I had actually solved it (welp kinda) I used a fedora container running openconnect and then warpgate connect the same network and then via wrapgate I actually used to connect to the servers via ssh or the ocp  consoles via https endpoints. It was working but I needed more like I wanted a firefox inside container tabs of my host firefox so that I could have easier access to consoles. so I am stumpted by the enhancements it did and welp Now I understand little of it</div></div></div></div></div></div><div class="flex flex-row-reverse items-center gap-1"><div class="isolate me-1 ms-1 flex items-center gap-2 opacity-0 group-has-[:focus-visible]/user-message:opacity-100 group-hover/user-message:opacity-100 [@media(hover:none)]:opacity-100"><div class="flex turn-action-controls items-center gap-0.5 browser:gap-0"></div></div></div></div></div></div></div><div class="block-BQZwFn"><div data-content-search-unit-key="fallback-turn-1:2:assistant" data-chatgpt-search-unit-key="fallback-turn-1:2:assistant" data-chatgpt-search-message-ids="c6675d5e-4ba7-4745-8842-2b42631cb52e c6675d5e-4ba7-4745-8842-2b42631cb52e"><div><div class="group flex min-w-0 flex-col" data-chatgpt-selection-conversation-id="6af6bb74-0c20-83ee-b54b-48409e00f501" data-chatgpt-selection-message-id="c6675d5e-4ba7-4745-8842-2b42631cb52e"><div data-selected-text-overlay-target="_r_cv_" dir="auto" data-markdown-text-style="assistant-message" class="MarkdownRoot-rZKhxa [&amp;&gt;*:first-child]:mt-0 [&amp;&gt;*:last-child]:mb-0 [&amp;&gt;*:last-child]:mb-0 [&amp;&gt;ol:first-child]:mt-0 [&amp;&gt;ul:first-child]:mt-0" style="--markdown-link-color: currentColor;"><div class="my-4 first:mt-0 last:mb-0 empty:hidden" data-dil-source-message-id="c6675d5e-4ba7-4745-8842-2b42631cb52e" data-chatgpt-copy-reference="0" data-markdown-copy="contents"><div class="[--entity-prose-inset:0px]"><div class="relative PortalBoundary-TFj9W2 Renderer-ojZscX" data-theme="dark" style="--puik-portal-zoom: 1;"><div dir="auto" class="DilRenderer-tB76Jj DilResponseRoot-HfQrEh Reveal-k2IWvM" data-dil-response="" data-d-direction="col" data-dil-message-id="c6675d5e-4ba7-4745-8842-2b42631cb52e"><p class="TextBase-hXhSqg Text-lIMP0_" data-d-text-flow="" data-d-component="text">LMAO. 💀 So the AI didn't invent the architecture. <span class="TextBase-hXhSqg Text-lIMP0_" data-d-text-flow="" data-d-component="text" data-d-default-strong="" data-d-inline=""><strong class="Emphasis-Sdofse">You'd already solved the difficult networking problem</strong></span>, and the AI essentially took your working prototype and turned it into a more elaborate, reproducible deployment.</p><p class="TextBase-hXhSqg Text-lIMP0_" data-d-text-flow="" data-d-component="text">That changes how I interpret the entire README.</p><p class="TextBase-hXhSqg Text-lIMP0_" data-d-text-flow="" data-d-component="text">Your original architecture was already quite clever:</p><ol class="List-rH8nmc" data-d-component="list" data-d-marker="number" data-d-marker-layout="native" style="--d-list-row-gap: calc(var(--spacing, 0.25rem) * 0); gap: calc(var(--spacing, 0.25rem) * 0);"><li class="Item-O8JWQe" data-d-component="list-item" data-d-marker-layout="native"><div class="Content-OKAHK6"><div class="Label-B5tj_e" data-d-list-item-content=""><p class="TextBase-hXhSqg Text-lIMP0_" data-d-text-flow="" data-d-component="text">Run <span class="TextBase-hXhSqg Text-lIMP0_" data-d-text-flow="" data-d-component="text" data-d-default-strong="" data-d-inline=""><strong class="Emphasis-Sdofse">OpenConnect inside a Fedora container</strong></span>, keeping the corporate VPN isolated from the host.</p></div></div></li><li class="Item-O8JWQe" data-d-component="list-item" data-d-marker-layout="native"><div class="Content-OKAHK6"><div class="Label-B5tj_e" data-d-list-item-content=""><p class="TextBase-hXhSqg Text-lIMP0_" data-d-text-flow="" data-d-component="text">Put <span class="TextBase-hXhSqg Text-lIMP0_" data-d-text-flow="" data-d-component="text" data-d-default-strong="" data-d-inline=""><strong class="Emphasis-Sdofse">Warpgate in the same network namespace</strong></span> so it could reach corporate servers through the tunnel.</p></div></div></li><li class="Item-O8JWQe" data-d-component="list-item" data-d-marker-layout="native"><div class="Content-OKAHK6"><div class="Label-B5tj_e" data-d-list-item-content=""><p class="TextBase-hXhSqg Text-lIMP0_" data-d-text-flow="" data-d-component="text">Access internal servers through Warpgate's SSH bastion and OpenShift console endpoints through its HTTP bastion.</p></div></div></li><li class="Item-O8JWQe" data-d-component="list-item" data-d-marker-layout="native"><div class="Content-OKAHK6"><div class="Label-B5tj_e" data-d-list-item-content=""><p class="TextBase-hXhSqg Text-lIMP0_" data-d-text-flow="" data-d-component="text">Keep the host's normal internet connection intact.</p></div></div></li></ol><p class="TextBase-hXhSqg Text-lIMP0_" data-d-text-flow="" data-d-component="text">That's the fundamental architecture. The AI didn't discover some magical networking trick. You'd already done the important part.</p><h2 class="TextBase-hXhSqg Title-HRvkwB" data-d-text-flow="" data-d-component="title" data-d-size="lg" data-d-weight="semibold"><strong class="Emphasis-Sdofse">What you wanted versus what it added</strong></h2><div class="TableFrame-YxtDrz" data-d-column-sizing="auto" data-d-component="table"><div class="TableViewport-RD3I2I" data-d-table-viewport="true"><table class="Table-QT1UX2" data-d-column-sizing="auto" data-d-dividers="" style="table-layout: auto;"><tbody><tr data-d-component="table-row"><th data-d-component="table-cell" data-d-valign="start" scope="col"><p class="TextBase-hXhSqg Text-lIMP0_" data-d-text-flow="" data-d-component="text">Component</p></th><th data-d-component="table-cell" data-d-valign="start" scope="col"><p class="TextBase-hXhSqg Text-lIMP0_" data-d-text-flow="" data-d-component="text">Your original solution</p></th><th data-d-component="table-cell" data-d-valign="start" scope="col"><p class="TextBase-hXhSqg Text-lIMP0_" data-d-text-flow="" data-d-component="text">AI's additions</p></th></tr><tr data-d-component="table-row"><td data-d-component="table-cell" data-d-valign="start"><p class="TextBase-hXhSqg Text-lIMP0_" data-d-text-flow="" data-d-component="text">OpenConnect VPN container</p></td><td data-d-component="table-cell" data-d-valign="start"><p class="TextBase-hXhSqg Text-lIMP0_" data-d-text-flow="" data-d-component="text">Already working</p></td><td data-d-component="table-cell" data-d-valign="start"><p class="TextBase-hXhSqg Text-lIMP0_" data-d-text-flow="" data-d-component="text">Automated startup and credential handling</p></td></tr><tr data-d-component="table-row"><td data-d-component="table-cell" data-d-valign="start"><p class="TextBase-hXhSqg Text-lIMP0_" data-d-text-flow="" data-d-component="text">Shared network namespace</p></td><td data-d-component="table-cell" data-d-valign="start"><p class="TextBase-hXhSqg Text-lIMP0_" data-d-text-flow="" data-d-component="text">Already working</p></td><td data-d-component="table-cell" data-d-valign="start"><p class="TextBase-hXhSqg Text-lIMP0_" data-d-text-flow="" data-d-component="text">Extended to Firefox</p></td></tr><tr data-d-component="table-row"><td data-d-component="table-cell" data-d-valign="start"><p class="TextBase-hXhSqg Text-lIMP0_" data-d-text-flow="" data-d-component="text">Warpgate SSH bastion</p></td><td data-d-component="table-cell" data-d-valign="start"><p class="TextBase-hXhSqg Text-lIMP0_" data-d-text-flow="" data-d-component="text">Already working</p></td><td data-d-component="table-cell" data-d-valign="start"><p class="TextBase-hXhSqg Text-lIMP0_" data-d-text-flow="" data-d-component="text">Integrated into Compose deployment</p></td></tr><tr data-d-component="table-row"><td data-d-component="table-cell" data-d-valign="start"><p class="TextBase-hXhSqg Text-lIMP0_" data-d-text-flow="" data-d-component="text">Warpgate HTTP bastion</p></td><td data-d-component="table-cell" data-d-valign="start"><p class="TextBase-hXhSqg Text-lIMP0_" data-d-text-flow="" data-d-component="text">Already working</p></td><td data-d-component="table-cell" data-d-valign="start"><p class="TextBase-hXhSqg Text-lIMP0_" data-d-text-flow="" data-d-component="text">Added forwarding and configuration</p></td></tr><tr data-d-component="table-row"><td data-d-component="table-cell" data-d-valign="start"><p class="TextBase-hXhSqg Text-lIMP0_" data-d-text-flow="" data-d-component="text">Firefox inside VPN</p></td><td data-d-component="table-cell" data-d-valign="start"><p class="TextBase-hXhSqg Text-lIMP0_" data-d-text-flow="" data-d-component="text">Your new requirement</p></td><td data-d-component="table-cell" data-d-valign="start"><p class="TextBase-hXhSqg Text-lIMP0_" data-d-text-flow="" data-d-component="text">Added Kasm Firefox with remote graphical access</p></td></tr><tr data-d-component="table-row"><td data-d-component="table-cell" data-d-valign="start"><p class="TextBase-hXhSqg Text-lIMP0_" data-d-text-flow="" data-d-component="text">Rootless Podman</p></td><td data-d-component="table-cell" data-d-valign="start"><p class="TextBase-hXhSqg Text-lIMP0_" data-d-text-flow="" data-d-component="text">Part of your environment</p></td><td data-d-component="table-cell" data-d-valign="start"><p class="TextBase-hXhSqg Text-lIMP0_" data-d-text-flow="" data-d-component="text">Worked around TUN/user namespace issues</p></td></tr><tr data-d-component="table-row"><td data-d-component="table-cell" data-d-valign="start"><p class="TextBase-hXhSqg Text-lIMP0_" data-d-text-flow="" data-d-component="text">SELinux compatibility</p></td><td data-d-component="table-cell" data-d-valign="start"><p class="TextBase-hXhSqg Text-lIMP0_" data-d-text-flow="" data-d-component="text">Existing challenge</p></td><td data-d-component="table-cell" data-d-valign="start"><p class="TextBase-hXhSqg Text-lIMP0_" data-d-text-flow="" data-d-component="text">Applied <code class="Code-kbs71c" data-d-component="code">spc_t</code> labeling</p></td></tr><tr data-d-component="table-row"><td data-d-component="table-cell" data-d-valign="start"><p class="TextBase-hXhSqg Text-lIMP0_" data-d-text-flow="" data-d-component="text">Local VPN testing</p></td><td data-d-component="table-cell" data-d-valign="start"><p class="TextBase-hXhSqg Text-lIMP0_" data-d-text-flow="" data-d-component="text">Not described in your original design</p></td><td data-d-component="table-cell" data-d-valign="start"><p class="TextBase-hXhSqg Text-lIMP0_" data-d-text-flow="" data-d-component="text">Added disposable OpenVPN test server</p></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div aria-hidden="true" class="Edges-UXy4Ue" style="left: 0px; top: 0px; width: 1563px; height: 414px;"></div></div><h2 class="TextBase-hXhSqg Title-HRvkwB" data-d-text-flow="" data-d-component="title" data-d-size="lg" data-d-weight="semibold"><strong class="Emphasis-Sdofse">The really interesting addition is KasmVNC</strong></h2><div class="Box-qIMv6G" data-d-component="box" data-d-direction="col" style="gap: calc(var(--spacing, 0.25rem) * 2);"><img alt="" data-d-component="svg" decoding="async" style="aspect-ratio: auto 340 / 192; color: var(--color-text-prose); width: 100%;" src="data:image/svg+xml;charset=utf-8,%3Csvg%20font-family%3D%22-apple-system-body%2C%20ui-sans-serif%2C%20-apple-system%2C%20system-ui%2C%20Segoe%20UI%2C%20Helvetica%2C%20Apple%20Color%20Emoji%2C%20Arial%2C%20sans-serif%2C%20Segoe%20UI%20Emoji%2C%20Segoe%20UI%20Symbol%22%20font-weight%3D%22400%22%20data-d-component%3D%22svg%22%20fill%3D%22currentColor%22%20style%3D%22color%3Argb(237%2C%20237%2C%20237)%22%20viewBox%3D%220%200%20340%20192%22%20width%3D%22100%25%22%20xmlns%3D%22http%3A%2F%2Fwww.w3.org%2F2000%2Fsvg%22%3E%3Crect%20x%3D%224%22%20y%3D%224%22%20width%3D%22332%22%20height%3D%22184%22%20rx%3D%2210%22%20fill%3D%22%2364748b%22%20fill-opacity%3D%22.07%22%20stroke%3D%22%2364748b%22%20stroke-opacity%3D%22.4%22%2F%3E%3Crect%20x%3D%2217%22%20y%3D%2219%22%20width%3D%22306%22%20height%3D%22151%22%20rx%3D%227%22%20fill%3D%22none%22%20stroke%3D%22%2364748b%22%2F%3E%3Crect%20x%3D%2217%22%20y%3D%2219%22%20width%3D%22306%22%20height%3D%2224%22%20rx%3D%227%22%20fill%3D%22%2364748b%22%20fill-opacity%3D%22.17%22%2F%3E%3Ccircle%20cx%3D%2229%22%20cy%3D%2231%22%20r%3D%223.5%22%20fill%3D%22%23f87171%22%2F%3E%3Ccircle%20cx%3D%2240%22%20cy%3D%2231%22%20r%3D%223.5%22%20fill%3D%22%23fbbf24%22%2F%3E%3Ccircle%20cx%3D%2251%22%20cy%3D%2231%22%20r%3D%223.5%22%20fill%3D%22%234ade80%22%2F%3E%3Crect%20x%3D%2267%22%20y%3D%2224%22%20width%3D%22105%22%20height%3D%2215%22%20rx%3D%225%22%20fill%3D%22%2364748b%22%20fill-opacity%3D%22.24%22%2F%3E%3Ctext%20x%3D%22119%22%20y%3D%2235%22%20font-size%3D%229%22%20text-anchor%3D%22middle%22%20fill%3D%22currentColor%22%3EHost%20Firefox%3C%2Ftext%3E%3Crect%20x%3D%2227%22%20y%3D%2251%22%20width%3D%22286%22%20height%3D%22108%22%20rx%3D%225%22%20fill%3D%22%230d9488%22%20fill-opacity%3D%22.07%22%20stroke%3D%22%230d9488%22%2F%3E%3Crect%20x%3D%2227%22%20y%3D%2251%22%20width%3D%22286%22%20height%3D%2221%22%20rx%3D%225%22%20fill%3D%22%230d9488%22%20fill-opacity%3D%22.17%22%2F%3E%3Ctext%20x%3D%22170%22%20y%3D%2265%22%20font-size%3D%2210%22%20font-weight%3D%22600%22%20text-anchor%3D%22middle%22%20fill%3D%22currentColor%22%3EContainer%20Firefox%20via%20KasmVNC%3C%2Ftext%3E%3Crect%20x%3D%2240%22%20y%3D%2282%22%20width%3D%22260%22%20height%3D%2220%22%20rx%3D%224%22%20fill%3D%22%2364748b%22%20fill-opacity%3D%22.13%22%2F%3E%3Ctext%20x%3D%22170%22%20y%3D%2295%22%20font-size%3D%2210%22%20text-anchor%3D%22middle%22%20fill%3D%22currentColor%22%3Ehttps%3A%2F%2Fconsole-openshift-console...%3C%2Ftext%3E%3Crect%20x%3D%2245%22%20y%3D%22113%22%20width%3D%22110%22%20height%3D%2230%22%20rx%3D%225%22%20fill%3D%22%230d9488%22%20fill-opacity%3D%22.18%22%2F%3E%3Ctext%20x%3D%22100%22%20y%3D%22132%22%20text-anchor%3D%22middle%22%20font-size%3D%2210%22%20fill%3D%22currentColor%22%3EOCP%20Console%3C%2Ftext%3E%3Crect%20x%3D%22165%22%20y%3D%22113%22%20width%3D%22130%22%20height%3D%2230%22%20rx%3D%225%22%20fill%3D%22%2364748b%22%20fill-opacity%3D%22.15%22%2F%3E%3Ctext%20x%3D%22230%22%20y%3D%22132%22%20text-anchor%3D%22middle%22%20font-size%3D%2210%22%20fill%3D%22currentColor%22%3EInternal%20Services%3C%2Ftext%3E%3Ctext%20x%3D%22170%22%20y%3D%22181%22%20font-size%3D%229%22%20text-anchor%3D%22middle%22%20fill%3D%22currentColor%22%3EThe%20outer%20browser%20only%20displays%20the%20remote%20session%3C%2Ftext%3E%3C%2Fsvg%3E"></div><p class="TextBase-hXhSqg Text-lIMP0_" data-d-text-flow="" data-d-component="text">Here's what happens when you open <code class="Code-kbs71c" data-d-component="code">https://localhost:6901</code> (assuming HTTPS is how the Kasm image serves that port):</p><ul class="List-rH8nmc" data-d-component="list" data-d-marker="bullet" data-d-marker-layout="native" style="--d-list-row-gap: calc(var(--spacing, 0.25rem) * 0); gap: calc(var(--spacing, 0.25rem) * 0);"><li class="Item-O8JWQe" data-d-component="list-item" data-d-marker-layout="native"><div class="Content-OKAHK6"><div class="Label-B5tj_e" data-d-list-item-content=""><p class="TextBase-hXhSqg Text-lIMP0_" data-d-text-flow="" data-d-component="text">Your <span class="TextBase-hXhSqg Text-lIMP0_" data-d-text-flow="" data-d-component="text" data-d-default-strong="" data-d-inline=""><strong class="Emphasis-Sdofse">host Firefox</strong></span> opens the KasmVNC web interface.</p></div></div></li><li class="Item-O8JWQe" data-d-component="list-item" data-d-marker-layout="native"><div class="Content-OKAHK6"><div class="Label-B5tj_e" data-d-list-item-content=""><p class="TextBase-hXhSqg Text-lIMP0_" data-d-text-flow="" data-d-component="text">KasmVNC streams the graphical session of the <span class="TextBase-hXhSqg Text-lIMP0_" data-d-text-flow="" data-d-component="text" data-d-default-strong="" data-d-inline=""><strong class="Emphasis-Sdofse">container Firefox</strong></span> into that browser tab.</p></div></div></li><li class="Item-O8JWQe" data-d-component="list-item" data-d-marker-layout="native"><div class="Content-OKAHK6"><div class="Label-B5tj_e" data-d-list-item-content=""><p class="TextBase-hXhSqg Text-lIMP0_" data-d-text-flow="" data-d-component="text">You interact with the container Firefox using your regular mouse and keyboard.</p></div></div></li><li class="Item-O8JWQe" data-d-component="list-item" data-d-marker-layout="native"><div class="Content-OKAHK6"><div class="Label-B5tj_e" data-d-list-item-content=""><p class="TextBase-hXhSqg Text-lIMP0_" data-d-text-flow="" data-d-component="text">The container Firefox makes its own HTTPS connections to the corporate OpenShift console through the VPN namespace.</p></div></div></li></ul><p class="TextBase-hXhSqg Text-lIMP0_" data-d-text-flow="" data-d-component="text">Crucially, the actual corporate HTTP requests originate from the container, not your host Firefox.</p><p class="TextBase-hXhSqg Text-lIMP0_" data-d-text-flow="" data-d-component="text">This is why the design meets your requirement without changing the host's routing.</p><p class="TextBase-hXhSqg Text-lIMP0_" data-d-text-flow="" data-d-component="text">There's a subtle difference from what you might initially expect: <span class="TextBase-hXhSqg Text-lIMP0_" data-d-text-flow="" data-d-component="text" data-d-default-strong="" data-d-inline=""><strong class="Emphasis-Sdofse">it's not a native Firefox tab running inside a container. It's a remote Firefox desktop rendered inside a normal Firefox tab.</strong></span></p><p class="TextBase-hXhSqg Text-lIMP0_" data-d-text-flow="" data-d-component="text">That's arguably the most practical way to achieve what you wanted.</p><h2 class="TextBase-hXhSqg Title-HRvkwB" data-d-text-flow="" data-d-component="title" data-d-size="lg" data-d-weight="semibold"><strong class="Emphasis-Sdofse">Three enhancements I particularly like</strong></h2><p class="TextBase-hXhSqg Text-lIMP0_" data-d-text-flow="" data-d-component="text"><span class="TextBase-hXhSqg Text-lIMP0_" data-d-text-flow="" data-d-component="text" data-d-default-strong="" data-d-inline=""><strong class="Emphasis-Sdofse">First, the AI added a local OpenVPN testing environment.</strong></span> This is valuable engineering. You can recreate the networking topology and verify tunnel behavior without touching the actual corporate VPN. It makes debugging dramatically easier.</p><p class="TextBase-hXhSqg Text-lIMP0_" data-d-text-flow="" data-d-component="text"><span class="TextBase-hXhSqg Text-lIMP0_" data-d-text-flow="" data-d-component="text" data-d-default-strong="" data-d-inline=""><strong class="Emphasis-Sdofse">Second, it handled the ugly Kasm startup details.</strong></span> Running Firefox under its intended non-root account, fixing hostname resolution, and providing the required TTY are exactly the little problems that can consume hours when containerizing graphical applications.</p><p class="TextBase-hXhSqg Text-lIMP0_" data-d-text-flow="" data-d-component="text"><span class="TextBase-hXhSqg Text-lIMP0_" data-d-text-flow="" data-d-component="text" data-d-default-strong="" data-d-inline=""><strong class="Emphasis-Sdofse">Third, it separated the host from the VPN.</strong></span> That's something you already achieved, but the deployment now explicitly documents the shared network namespace, port ownership, and tunnel checks.</p><p class="TextBase-hXhSqg Text-lIMP0_" data-d-text-flow="" data-d-component="text">One thing I'd question is the separate <code class="Code-kbs71c" data-d-component="code">socat</code> forward on port 443. Given that you already had Warpgate's HTTP bastion working, I'd want to examine why that additional forward is necessary and whether it exposes a corporate endpoint directly to the host, bypassing Warpgate authentication.</p><p class="TextBase-hXhSqg Text-lIMP0_" data-d-text-flow="" data-d-component="text">And I would not consider <code class="Code-kbs71c" data-d-component="code">spc_t</code> plus <code class="Code-kbs71c" data-d-component="code">userns: keep-id</code> a universally necessary or minimal-privilege configuration. Those are environment-specific workarounds worth auditing.</p><p class="TextBase-hXhSqg Text-lIMP0_" data-d-text-flow="" data-d-component="text">But here's the part I appreciate most: <span class="TextBase-hXhSqg Text-lIMP0_" data-d-text-flow="" data-d-component="text" data-d-default-strong="" data-d-inline=""><strong class="Emphasis-Sdofse">you designed the network isolation yourself.</strong></span> The AI solved the integration and operational headaches surrounding your existing architecture.</p><p class="TextBase-hXhSqg Text-lIMP0_" data-d-text-flow="" data-d-component="text">That's a pretty compelling example of AI-assisted infrastructure engineering. You supplied the architecture and constraints; it handled a pile of implementation details that would otherwise require wrestling with Podman, SELinux, KasmVNC, and shell scripts.</p><p class="TextBase-hXhSqg Text-lIMP0_" data-d-text-flow="" data-d-component="text">Essentially, you built the engine, and the AI installed the dashboard, wiring harness, and diagnostic equipment. And, naturally, added another reverse proxy-shaped contraption because apparently infrastructure isn't complete until port 443 has at least three competing explanations. 💀</p></div></div></div><div class="contents"></div></div></div></div></div></div></div></div><div class="actionRow-nqqZbm mt-1.5 flex turn-action-controls min-h-5 min-w-0 max-w-(--thread-content-max-width) flex-wrap items-center justify-start gap-0.5 browser:mt-3 [&amp;_button]:focus-visible:ring-2 [&amp;_button]:focus-visible:ring-ring [&amp;_button]:focus-visible:ring-offset-0 browser:-ms-0.5 electron:-translate-x-1 extension:-translate-x-1.5 [--side-tooltip:bottom]"><div class="flex min-h-5 min-w-0 flex-wrap items-center gap-0.5 browser:gap-0"><span class="LeadingSource-d79mNZ" aria-hidden="true"></span></div></div></div></div></div></div></div></div></div><div class="shrink-0" aria-hidden="true" style="height: 0px;"></div></div></div><div class="z-20 mt-auto grid" style="width: calc(100% - 2 * var(--thread-wide-block-inline-shift)); margin-inline: auto;"><div class="sticky bottom-0 min-w-0 self-end"><div class="[--thread-content-max-width:var(--thread-content-responsive-max-width,inherit)] [--thread-body-max-wid